<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Friday!</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Identify independently common actions seen in daily routines. Interactive: Participate in simple formulaic question-and-answer exchanges on familiar subjects.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify common actions using simple phrases (e.g., "I write," "I read," and "I listen."). Can participate in simple question-and-answer exchanges about days of the week (e.g., "What day is it today?" "Today is Tuesday." "What day is tomorrow?" "Tomorrow is Wednesday."). Can describe common actions in daily routines in simple exchanges. (e.g., "We clean up on Monday." "We sing on Tuesday." "I read on Wednesday.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 25px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone! Let's sing our 'Days of the Week' song!" The teacher leads a familiar song about the days of the week (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday) with accompanying TPR actions. As each day is sung, the teacher shows a flashcard of the day and an action. For example, for "Monday," the teacher shows a picture of "learn" and says "On Monday, we learn math!" For "Friday," the teacher shows "draw" and says "On Friday, we draw!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a calendar flashcard. "Look! This is our calendar." The teacher points to "today." "Today is Friday!" (emphasizing /f/). "Yesterday was Thursday." The teacher points to "tomorrow." "Tomorrow is Saturday." The teacher then shows flashcards for actions (read, write, listen, draw, sing) and models simple sentences: "I read," "I write," "I listen." The teacher ensures clear pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" through repetition and exaggeration.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and gestures to check understanding.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to "Friday" on the calendar) "Is today Monday?" (Students shake heads or say "No.") "What day is it today?" (Students respond: "Friday!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a book flashcard) "Is 'read' for drawing?" (Students shake heads or say "No.") "What is 'read' for?" (Students might point to the book or say "book.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "Our School Schedule" chart displayed in the classroom. The chart lists the days of the week (Monday-Friday) and features simple pictures next to each day showing common school activities (e.g., Monday: math, read; Tuesday: science, write; Wednesday: English, listen; Thursday: draw, share; Friday: sing, play with friends). It also includes a visual for 'sunny', 'rainy', 'cloudy' for each day's weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Talking about schedules: Communicating about daily classroom activities and schedules using appropriate language and turn-taking (e.g., "Today is Monday."). Answering questions about days: "Yesterday was Wednesday." Expressing preferences: "I like Friday. Art is on Friday!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the chart. "Look at our school schedule for the week!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (Choral Response):</b> "What day is it today?" (Students: "Friday!") "What do we do on Monday?" (Students look at the chart and say "Math!" "Read!") "What do we do on Tuesday?" (Students: "Science!" "Write!") The teacher models the full sentence: "On Tuesday, we write." Students repeat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Pointing):</b> The teacher asks, "Who can point to the day we 'sing'?" Students point to Friday. "Who can point to the day we 'listen'?" Students point to Wednesday.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Thumbs Up/Down):</b> "On Wednesday, we draw." (Students give thumbs down.) "On Friday, we sing." (Students give thumbs up.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Past simple with "be" (e.g., "Yesterday was..."), Present simple (e.g., "Today is..."), and WH-questions (e.g., "What day is it? What do we...?"). Vocabulary includes: Monday-Friday, school, friend, math, science, English, be, learn, read, write, say, listen, share, talk, draw, like, have, yesterday, today, tomorrow, on.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Day Chain Drill (Choral and Individual)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher starts by saying, "Today is Friday." Then points to a student. "What day was yesterday?" (Student: "Yesterday was Thursday.") Teacher points to another student. "What day is tomorrow?" (Student: "Tomorrow is Saturday.") This continues around the class, reinforcing the sequence of days and the target grammar. The teacher ensures correct pronunciation of /w/ and /f/. For example, the teacher might say, "Listen! /w/ in Wednesday, /f/ in Friday." and have students repeat.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "On [Day], We [Action]" Flashcard Drill (Choral and Pair Work)</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher holds up a flashcard of a day (e.g., "Tuesday") and an action (e.g., "write"). Students chorally respond, "On Tuesday, we write." The teacher then mixes and matches flashcards. Next, students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of day and action flashcards. Student A picks a day and an action, asks "What do we do on [day]?" Student B responds, "On [day], we [action]." They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation.</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction: "I Like [Day]!"</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are paired up. Each pair has a simplified "School Schedule" (similar to the one presented but smaller). Student A asks Student B, "What day do you like?" Student B answers, "I like [day] because we [activity]!" (e.g., "I like Friday because we draw!"). Student A then asks, "What day is it today?" Student B answers, "Today is Friday." They switch roles. The teacher encourages using "and" to add more information (e.g., "I like Friday and we play with friends!").</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Favorite School Day" Mini-Presentation (Pair Work)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in pairs to prepare a short oral presentation about their favorite school day and the activities they do. They will use the target language to describe their routine. Each pair receives a large piece of paper or a small whiteboard to draw two or three pictures representing activities on their chosen favorite day. For example, if they like Friday, they might draw a picture of themselves singing and another of them drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one school day they like the most (e.g., "I like Friday!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw 2-3 simple pictures of activities they do on that day (e.g., 'sing', 'draw', 'play with friends').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They practice saying sentences about their day using the sentence frames:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like [day]!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"On [day], we [activity 1] and we [activity 2]." (e.g., "On Friday, we sing and we draw.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each pair presents their "Favorite School Day" to another pair or to the whole class, showing their drawings and speaking their sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages other students to ask simple questions like, "What day is it?" or "What do you do on [day]?" and prompts the presenting students to answer.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher will use a simple checklist to observe students' speaking during the "My Favorite School Day" mini-presentations, noting their ability to use target vocabulary and grammatical structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will present their chosen day and activities to their partner or the class, verbally identifying the day and at least two activities using simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least three vocabulary words (days of the week, verbs like read, write, sing, draw).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and participation:</b> Students speak clearly and participate in the exchange (e.g., "I like Friday," "On Friday, we sing and we draw.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students work together in pairs to prepare and present.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a smiley face/sad face option) "Can I say 'Today is Friday'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a thumbs up/down option) "Can I say 'On Monday, we read'?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today?" (e.g., "Friday!" "Draw!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about talking about your school day?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The use of flashcards and the "Day Chain Drill" helped students recall days and practice "was/is." The pair work for "On [Day], we [Action]" encouraged active participation. Students enjoyed talking about their favorite day and drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still confuse "yesterday" and "tomorrow." More visual aids or a timeline might be helpful. Pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" needs continued reinforcement. For the next lesson, we can transition to writing by giving them sentence starters from this lesson's oral practice.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use the sentences and vocabulary practiced today to write simple sentences and short paragraphs about their daily school routines and favorite days. The drawings from today's production task can serve as a visual guide for their writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>